ojs - okernel's JavaScript engine
=================================

Written from scratch for okernel (no third-party code). It runs page scripts
for the okai browser (src/web/wjs*.c embeds it, one realm per document) and
builds the same on the host for testing (tests/ojs/build.sh).

Layout
------
  ojs.h            embedding API (values, eval/call, objects, roots, modules)
  ojs_int.h        internal types: values, strings, objects, shapes, intrinsics
  gc.c             heap: size-class pages + large blocks, non-moving mark-sweep
                   with a conservative scan of the C stack and of VM frames
  str.c            strings (latin-1 / UTF-16, ropes), atoms, string builder
  obj.c            ordinary objects: shapes (shared transitions, dictionaries),
                   fast elements, property definitions
  lex.c parse.c parse_stmt.c   source -> AST (scopes, early errors)
  compile*.c       AST -> bytecode (per-function templates, line tables)
  vm.c vm_run.c vm_helpers.c   the interpreter (direct-threaded, inline caches)
  realm.c b_*.c generator.c module.c   the built-ins, generators/async, modules
  re.c b_regexp.c  regular expressions (backtracking matcher, v-mode sets)
  num.c bigint.c   number <-> string (exact), BigInt
  srctext.c        compact (UTF-8) source text kept for compiled functions
  unicode.c unicode_data.c     Unicode tables; unicode_data.c is generated
                   from the Unicode Character Database by tools/gen_unicode.py
                   (data, not code)

Things to know
--------------
* Values are NaN-boxed 64-bit words (ojsv). No reference counting: the
  collector scans the C stack between ojs_enter() and the current frame, and
  the VM value stack conservatively; C heap memory holding values must be
  registered (ojs_add_root / ojs_add_root_range).
* x87 only, like the kernel: double -> int conversions go through the IEEE
  bits (d2i64, dtoi32, jv_number) because a C cast changes the x87 rounding
  mode twice per conversion.
* Inline caches (struct ic, per property/global site) are made on first use
  and dropped by every collection, so they never point at freed objects.
* The realm memory limit (ojs_set_memory_limit) counts memory taken from the
  system; an allocation that would pass it collects first.
* Templates keep their script's source (toString, stack traces). A big script
  that needed UTF-16 is re-stored as UTF-8 after compiling (srctext.c), so it
  costs about its UTF-8 size, not twice that.
* 64-bit division in the kernel comes from src/divdi3.c (no libgcc).

Tests
-----
  sh tests/ojs/build.sh                 host build -> build-host/ojs_run
  ./build-host/ojs_run tests/ojs/smoke.js           (prints ALL OK)
  python3 tests/ojs/run262.py -j8 test/language     test262 (~/test262)
  python3 tests/ojs/corpus/run_corpus.py            real libraries, node vs ojs
  OJS_GCSTRESS=N   collect every N allocations (any runner)
  OJS_GCSTATS=1    heap / live bytes by type at exit
  OJS_DUMP=1       bytecode listing
  OJS_MEMLIMIT=MB  realm limit in ojs_run (like a page)
The corpus downloads third-party libraries into build-host/corpus-lib as test
input only; they are never part of the kernel.
